BCM2837 hardcodes the panic log to a Raspberry Pi 3 physical address.

pexpert/pexpert/arm64/BCM2837.h sets:

    #define PLATFORM_PANIC_LOG_PADDR        0x3c0fc000
    #define PLATFORM_PANIC_LOG_SIZE         16384

and pexpert/arm/pe_init.c's check_for_panic_log() uses them unconditionally
when they exist:

    #ifdef PLATFORM_PANIC_LOG_PADDR
        gPanicBase = ml_io_map_wcomb(PLATFORM_PANIC_LOG_PADDR, PLATFORM_PANIC_LOG_SIZE);
        ...
    #else
        /* read the /pram node's reg and /chosen's embedded-panic-log-size */
    #endif

0x3c0fc000 is where the Pi 3's VideoCore firmware leaves its region, below
the 0x40000000 ARM DRAM split. It is a property of one specific board, not
of the SoC family, and on any other board it is some other device's
register space -- or, as here, not DRAM at all. Writing a panic log there
either faults or corrupts an unrelated peripheral, and reading it back on
the next boot yields garbage that check_for_panic_log() may then mistake
for a real panic header or an 'SHMC' shared-memory console.

The fix is to delete the two defines, nothing more. The #else branch is
already the general implementation and needs no new code: it takes the
region's base from the device tree's /pram node (reg[0]) and its length
from /chosen's embedded-panic-log-size. That is how every Apple target
does it, and it lets each board place the region wherever its own memory
map has room.

So the board supplying the device tree must provide:

    /pram          reg                       = { base, length }   (two u64)
    /chosen        embedded-panic-log-size   = length             (u32)

and both must agree on the length. If either is missing the function
returns early, leaving gPanicBase NULL -- no panic log, but no fault
either, which is a safe default for a board still being brought up.

Place the region outside the memory the kernel manages, i.e. below
boot_args.physBase. A page inside the managed range gets handed out as
ordinary memory while also being mapped write-combine here, which both
aliases mismatched attributes and lets the allocator hand the panic
buffer to something else.

--- a/pexpert/pexpert/arm64/BCM2837.h
+++ b/pexpert/pexpert/arm64/BCM2837.h
@@ -76,8 +76,12 @@
 #define BCM2837_PUT32(addr, value) do { *((volatile uint32_t *) addr) = value; } while(0)
 #define BCM2837_GET32(addr) *((volatile uint32_t *) addr)

-#define PLATFORM_PANIC_LOG_PADDR        0x3c0fc000
-#define PLATFORM_PANIC_LOG_SIZE         16384        // 16kb
+/*
+ * No PLATFORM_PANIC_LOG_PADDR here on purpose. 0x3c0fc000 is the Pi 3's own
+ * address and is wrong for every other board using this config, so
+ * check_for_panic_log() in pexpert/arm/pe_init.c takes the region from the
+ * device tree instead: /pram's reg and /chosen's embedded-panic-log-size.
+ */
 #endif /* ! ASSEMBLER */

 #endif /* ! _PEXPERT_ARM_BCM2837_H */
